catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 05 — talking to C, and mocking it.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D03 §7, D-49, D-57): a foreign call is an operation of an
\ ordinary effect. Nothing about it is special-cased, so everything demos 02
\ and 03 showed applies to it — including that you can HANDLE it. Testing code
\ that calls libc needs no test double, no link-time seam and no build variant.
\
\ Two effects appear in every `extern`'s row and they say different things:
\   !C      — this talks to C. Honest, and worth keeping in the type.
\   !Unsafe — nobody has checked the memory safety of the call.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- Declaring a foreign function. The word name IS the C symbol. The
\ signature is mandatory — there is no body to infer one from — and the row is
\ not written, because it is always the same two effects.
catcat> extern strlen ( str -- i64 )
extern abs    ( i64 -- i64 )
extern getenv ( str -- str )
extern getpid ( -- i64 )
extern time   ( -- i64 )
extern strlen ( str -- i64 !C !Unsafe )
extern abs ( i64 -- i64 !C !Unsafe )
extern getenv ( str -- str !C !Unsafe )
extern getpid ( -- i64 !C !Unsafe )
extern time ( -- i64 !C !Unsafe )
catcat> \ That is libc, linked into this binary. Not an imitation of it:
catcat> unsafe { "hello, world" strlen } show print "\n" print
unsafe { -5 abs } show print "\n" print
12
5
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Only `i64` and `str` cross the boundary, and that is checked where the
\ `extern` is written rather than where it is called:
catcat> extern f ( bool -- )
error: extern f: only i64 and str cross the C boundary so far
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Unsafety propagates, and the type system makes you say so
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ A word that calls one and claims to be clean is rejected:
catcat> define name_size ( str -- i64 ! ) { strlen }
error: name_size declares no effects but its body has !C !Unsafe
catcat> \ `unsafe { … }` is how you vouch for the call. It is an effect with no
\ operations, so discharging it is a claim and not a check — exactly as much
\ of a promise as the word says it is. Note what survives: `!C` stays, because
\ vouching for the memory safety of a call does not stop it being a call.
catcat> define name_size ( str -- i64 !C ) { unsafe { strlen } }
defined name_size ( str -- i64 !C )
catcat> "catcat" name_size show print "\n" print
6
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ `unsafe` is a handler, not a keyword with a compiler rule behind it:
catcat> locate name_size
define name_size ( str -- i64 !C ) {
  handle Unsafe over (  ) init { } { } { strlen }
}
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ The point: a deterministic test of code that calls libc
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Something worth testing, and hard to test. It reads the environment, asks
\ the OS for a pid and asks the clock for the time, so it answers differently
\ on every machine and in every second.
catcat> define session_tag ( -- i64 !C ) {
  unsafe { "HOME" getenv strlen getpid + time + }
}
defined session_tag ( -- i64 !C )
catcat> \ This file's output is checked against a golden transcript by `make demos`,
\ so it cannot print that number — which is precisely the problem. Call it and
\ throw the answer away:
catcat> session_tag pop
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Now handle `C`. One handler, four implementations, and the program under
\ test is untouched: `session_tag` was compiled against the real symbols and
\ knows nothing about this.
catcat> define fixed ( -- i64 ) {
  handle C over ( ) init { } {
    getenv { pop "/home/tester" }
    strlen { pop 12 }
    getpid { 4242 }
    time   { 1700000000 }
  } { session_tag }
}
defined fixed ( -- i64 )
catcat> "mocked: " print fixed show print "\n" print
"mocked: " print fixed show print "\n" print
mocked: 1700004254
mocked: 1700004254
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Twice the same, and the same on any machine — which is why those two lines
\ can live in a golden file and `session_tag`'s cannot.
catcat> \ Read `fixed`'s signature again: it is NOT `!C`. Handling the effect
\ discharged it, so the type records that this version does not talk to the
\ outside world. The test is typed as the pure computation it is.
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ What is missing here
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ `extern` reaches a fixed table of six libc symbols rather than `dlsym`,
\ because calling an arbitrary symbol needs libffi to build a call frame at
\ run time. Nothing on the catcat side would change if it did. A name outside
\ the table declares fine and is reported at the CALL, as an operation that
\ escaped — which is exactly what it is:
catcat> extern nosuchfn ( -- i64 )
extern nosuchfn ( -- i64 !C !Unsafe )
catcat> unsafe { nosuchfn }
unhandled: nosuchfn escaped with no handler in scope
catcat> \ And it can be mocked like any other, which is how you would develop against
\ a symbol the host does not have yet:
catcat> handle C over ( ) init { } { nosuchfn { 7 } } { unsafe { nosuchfn } }
show print "\n" print
7
ok  (empty)
